049186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049828 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049828 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049828 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049828 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049828 #edb/ref 1031 "Domain and Range of Quadratic Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049828 #edb/ref 1033 1882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049828 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "171bc42f-1e67-52f7-92c6-e9af227f8d0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049919 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049919 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049919 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049919 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049919 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049919 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049919 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049919 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049919 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049919 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049919 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049919 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049919 #edb/ref 1031 "Graphs of General Quadratic Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049919 #edb/ref 1033 84 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049919 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "1e7769c8-1985-591a-bba8-e917166ea21d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050142 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050142 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186172136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050142 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186172137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050142 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186172138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050142 #edb/ref 1031 "Writing the Equation of a Parabola in Vertex Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050142 #edb/ref 1033 3546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050142 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "30510c62-b4f5-5b4d-9d00-f64786d1c9a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087436 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1466" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087436 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=-3(x+5)^2-4$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087436 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c9e4edff-b04f-5f6c-8066-43cae6a653e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087436 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the vertex form of a parabola is\n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^2 + k\n$$\n\nwhere $(h,k)$ are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola.\n\nThe given parabola\n\n$$\ny=-3(x+5)^2-4\n$$\n\nis written in vertex form with $h=-5$ and $k=-4$. To see this clearly, note that we can write the parabola as follows:\n\n$$\ny=-3(x-(-5))^2-4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex of the parabola is at $(-5,-4)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087445 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1795" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087445 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=4(x-3)^2+2$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087445 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8b64498e-61fb-5eab-a97a-b321be8360ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087445 #edb/ref 1074 "The axis of symmetry of the parabola is the vertical line that passes through the vertex of the parabola. To find the axis of symmetry, we need to compute the $x$-coordinate of the vertex of the parabola.\n\nRemember that the vertex form of a parabola is\n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^2 + k\n$$\n\nwhere $(h,k)$ are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola.\n\nThe given parabola\n\n$$\ny=4(x-3)^2+2\n$$\n\nis written in vertex form with $h=3$ and $k=2$. Therefore, the vertex of the parabola is at $(3,2)$, and the axis of symmetry is $x=3$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087631 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7479" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087631 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the minimum value of the parabola $y=(x-5)^2 -4$, sketched below.\n\n![](images/e3/e360d812160d36bbc528ce65f0c8b19941e75ce8703204205a94967ecccdc892.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087631 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d4c1e4ea-da29-5027-bfc3-f909f9181cd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087631 #edb/ref 1074 "The minimum value of the parabola is the $y$-coordinate of the vertex of the parabola.\n\nRemember that the vertex form of a parabola is\n\n$$\ny = a(x-h)^2 + k\n$$\n\nwhere $(h,k)$ are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola.\n\nThe given parabola\n\n$$\ny = (x-5)^2 -4\n$$\n\nis written in vertex form with $h=5$ and $k=-4$. Therefore, the vertex of the parabola is at $(5,-4)$, and its minimum value is $-4$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087803 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087803 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087803 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Minimum or Maximum Value of a Parabola Given in Vertex Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087803 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087803 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "97f63d2a-8297-5034-96e0-ecae4f62c5a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087831 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087831 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087831 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087831 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087831 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Vertex of a Parabola Given in Vertex Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087831 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087831 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "61e9b29c-326d-576c-b998-ab975492ed85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087885 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087885 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087885 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Axis of Symmetry of a Parabola Given in Vertex Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087885 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087885 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b4018be9-18ab-5854-87d6-149238ab6f53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087900 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087900 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087900 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087900 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087900 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087900 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087900 #edb/ref 1045 "The Vertex Form of a Parabola" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087900 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186088745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087900 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087900 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d16c173d-d5fc-51c9-adf4-fe3999473ab4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088500 #edb/ref 1061 136678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088500 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c203d163-0475-523c-b583-dea9d27a9184" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088500 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088690 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088690 #edb/ref 1061 27764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088690 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a04762c2-835a-5511-af4c-a7041c880b62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088690 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088745 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088745 #edb/ref 1061 4592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088745 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cbd152b4-52af-56c9-8eab-5981824f4582" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088745 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088746 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088746 #edb/ref 1061 4593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088746 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e1a2056c-2d10-5b6e-adcd-9ea2956bbe34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088746 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088836 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088836 #edb/ref 1061 6181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088836 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c556f464-3107-5978-b675-fbf5f1c7c267" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088836 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088870 #edb/ref 1064 "Up to now, all the parabolas we've seen have been written in **standard form**.\n\n$$\ny = ax^2+bx+c\n$$\n\nWe can also write the equation of a parabola in the so-called **vertex form,** given by\n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^2 + k\n$$\n\nWhen a parabola is written in vertex form, the pair $(h,k)$ gives the coordinates of its vertex.\n\nFor example, consider the following parabola:\n\n$$\ny=(x-2)^2 +1\n$$\n\nThis parabola is written in vertex form with $a = 1, h=2$ and $k=1$. Therefore, the vertex of the parabola is at $(2,1)$.\n\n![](images/0c/0c53b8e5d2124d13d92dd81ab4868c6cc128da40eabfe51e684306bb0bbf0457.png)\n\nIn future lessons, we'll learn how to express any parabola in vertex form. For now, let's get some practice working with this form." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088870 #edb/ref 1065 1648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088870 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a7456ef9-dd1e-56e5-a24f-111ba9692968" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088870 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088990 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider our first example once more.\n\n![](images/0c/0c53b8e5d2124d13d92dd81ab4868c6cc128da40eabfe51e684306bb0bbf0457.png)\n\nYou might be wondering how we can tell that the vertex coordinates are $(2,1)$ just from looking at the equation.\n\nTo see this, let's label the two terms in our parabola formula:\n\n$$\ny=\\underbrace{(x-2)^2}_{\\textrm{quadratic part}}+\\underbrace{1}_{\\textrm{constant}}\n$$\n\nNow, note the following:\n\n- The quadratic part $(x-2)^2$ is a *squared* quantity. Therefore, it can only be *positive* or *zero*.\n- Moreover, the quadratic part *equals* zero when $x=2$.\n- Since the constant part $(1)$ does not vary with $x$, the entire expression is *minimized* when $x=2$. Therefore, the $x$-coordinate of the minimum point (i.e., the vertex) is $2$.\n- When $x=2$, the quadratic part is zero, giving $y=0^2+1 = 1$. Therefore, the $y$-coordinate of the vertex is $1$.\n\nFollowing this logic, we conclude that the coordinates of the vertex are $(2,1)$.\n\nFinally, it's possible to generalize this argument to any parabola. For $a < 0$, the coordinates $(h,k)$ give the maximum of the parabola." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088990 #edb/ref 1065 9115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088990 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d30b5d5a-0248-511a-9f7f-dfff00438889" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088990 #edb/ref 1067 "Why Does Vertex Form Give the Coordinates of the Vertex?" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
